# Why would a market ever be efficient?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is the \"invisible hand\" argument, step by step? Which conditions does it quietly need?"
next: {"title":"What exactly is Pareto efficiency?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/23-what-exactly-is-pareto-efficiency.cast"}

## Five fish, one price
use: bar_chart
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 110
      w: 660
      h: 550
    labels:
      - 1st
      - 2nd
      - 3rd
      - 4th
      - 5th
    series:
      - {name: Worth to buyer, values: [[10, 8, 6, 4, 2], [10, 8, 6, 4, 2]]}
      - {name: Cost to fisher, values: [[0, 0, 0, 0, 0], [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]]}
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    y_label: Dollars per fish
    reference:
      value: 5.5
      label: ''
      color: purple
sources:
    - id: smith1776
      title: An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations
      authors: Adam Smith
      year: 1776
      finding: 'Book I ch. 2: ''It is not from the benevolence of the butcher, the brewer, or the baker, that we expect our dinner, but from their regard to their own interest.'' Book IV ch. 2: the only use of ''invisible hand'' in the book — ''led by an invisible hand to promote an end which was no part of his intention.'''

    row trio gap 60 fit.h 160 fit.w 480 fit.x 260 fit.y 380 hidden true
    icon i_meat size 110 or ["meat"] icon_look picture of "cut of meat" in trio hidden true
    icon i_beer size 110 or ["beer"] icon_look picture of "beer mug" in trio hidden true
    icon i_bread size 110 icon_look picture of bread in trio hidden true
    portrait smith x 130 y 300 width 190 of "Adam Smith" hidden true
    text quote "“not from the benevolence of the butcher…”" cites ["smith1776"] x 560 y 270 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch calc work [{"tex":"(10+8+6)-(1+3+5)"},{"tex":"= \\$15"}] x 855 y 440 font_size 20 hidden true
    text ihand "“an invisible hand”" #8a5fa8 cites ["smith1776"] x 850 y 440 font_size 32 hidden true
    text key_b "■ Worth to buyer" #b5482e x 850 y 615 font_size 26 hidden true
    text key_f "■ Cost to fisher" #2f6b8f x 850 y 575 font_size 26 hidden true
    text key_p "- - Price $5.50" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 530 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Why would a market ever be efficient?"

    hide legend

Why would a market ever be efficient, when nobody in it is even trying to be?
    draw i_meat i_beer i_bread parallel true

B: Adam Smith put it this way in 1776: it is not from the benevole (@draw quote@)nce of the butcher, the brewer, or the baker, that we expect our dinner, but from their regard to their own interest.
    draw smith

Self-interest feeding a town is one thing. The bolder claim is that it feeds it well, with nothing wasted.
    erase smith quote i_meat i_beer i_bread

Let's test that on Harbourtown's fish quay.
    draw axes

B: Five buyers w (@draw key_b@)ant a fish today. Say, to keep it simple, the keenest would pay ten dollars, the next eight, then six, four and two.
    draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5

And five fi (@draw key_f@)shers could land one fish each. The cheapest catch costs one dollar, then three, five, seven and nine.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

    quiz "How many fish should change hands to make the total gain as big as possible?" judge false voice b
        intro: Now be a wise planner who knows every one of those numbers.
        right: Three fish: the first three are each worth more than they cost.
        * Two fish
        * Three fish
        * Four fish
        * All five

Ten against one, eight against three, six against five: each of those fish is worth more than it costs.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

The fourth would cost seven to land and is worth only four.
    highlight bar_4 effect box

B: So the planner's best is fifteen dollars of gain. Two fish give fourteen, four give twelve.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1 calc_line_2

    erase calc

Now send the planner home, and let a price settle on the quay. Say five dollars fifty.
    draw reference_1 key_p

B: The three buyers who value a fish above five fifty buy. The three fishers who can land one for less than that sell.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

Exactly the planner's three fish, and nobody needed to know anyone else's numbers.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 effect box

Smith's phrase for this was being led by an invisible hand, to promote an end which was no part of his intention.
    draw ihand

B: He used that phrase once in the whole book. Economists have spent two centuries working out when it is actually true.
    highlight ihand effect underline

## The argument, step by step
use: supply_demand
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 110
      w: 580
      h: 550
    x_label: Fish a day (Q)
    y_label: Price of a fish (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
      label: 'D: buyers'
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
      label: 'S: fishers'
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 1.5
      side: seller
      label: S′
    units:
      price: [0, 6]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: fish
    regions:
      - consumer_surplus
      - producer_surplus
      - deadweight_loss
    region_labels: false

    scratch steps work ["1  Buy if worth ≥ P","2  Sell if cost ≤ P","3  P moves: wanted = landed","4  Every gainful trade made"] x 805 y 430 font_size 24 hidden true
    text eff "Shaded: total gain, at its most" #3d3833 x 805 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\text{gain} = \\sum (\\text{worth} - \\text{cost})"},{"tex":"Q<Q^*:\\ \\text{next gap} > 0"},{"tex":"\\text{peak: worth} = MC"}] x 805 y 430 font_size 24 hidden true
    text cue_sub "Subsidy: $1 a fish" #2f6b8f x 805 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cue_tax "Tax: $1 a fish" #b5482e x 805 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cue_lost "red = gain lost" #c96567 x 805 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "The argument, step by step"

    hide price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps tax_guide_lines

Fifteen dollars on five fish is a toy. With hundreds of buyers and fishers, the steps of those bars smooth out into two curves.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

B: The argument has four steps. One: you buy a fish when it is worth at least the price. So the (@highlight demand_curve@) demand curve lines up buyers by what a fish is worth to them.
    draw steps_box steps_line_1

B: Two: a fisher sells when the price covers the cost of the catch. Th (@highlight supply_curve@)e supply curve lines up fishers by that cost.
    draw steps_line_2

B: Three: competition pushes the price until the fish wanted equal the fish landed.
    draw steps_line_3 equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

And four is the payoff?
    point at.ref equilibrium_point gesture circle

B: Four: every fish worth more than it costs gets sold, and no fish that costs more than it is worth.
    draw steps_line_4

B: The total gain, both triangles together, is as big as this market can make it.
    draw cs_region ps_region

    erase steps

That is what efficient means here: no trade worth making left undone, and none made that wasn't worth it.
    draw eff

    quiz "A dollar for every fish landed: does the total gain grow or shrink?" voice b
        intro: Test it. Say the council pays fishers a dollar for every fish landed, so more fish reach the town.
        right: It shrinks.
        wrong: Compare what the extra fish cost to land with what buyers would pay: here, it shrinks.
        * It grows
        + It shrinks
        * It stays the same

    erase eff cs_region ps_region

    draw tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point dwl_region

    draw cue_sub cue_lost

The extra fish cost more to land than anyone will pay for them, so a triangle of waste opens beyond the crossing.
    animate tax.amount -16.7 duration 3

B: And to (@erase cue_sub@)o few (@draw cue_tax@) fish is just as bad. A tax that cuts the catch loses fish that were worth catching, on the other side.
    animate tax.amount 16.7 duration 3

Try it: drag the taxed supply curve up or down. Any push away from the crossing costs a triangle.
    explore

    erase cue_tax cue_lost

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see why the total peaks exactly there?

@math

    erase mathq

Total gain is worth minus cost, added up over every fish sold.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Left of the crossing, the next fish adds a positive gap; right of it, a negative one.
    draw math_line_2

So the total peaks where the gap is zero: where the last buyer's worth equals the marginal cost, MC.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@after

    erase mathq

    pause 0.3

## Does it need clever traders?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Gode and Sunder would hire you, not the robots.
    good: Nearly all the gain. Like a random trader with a rule.
    poor: Even random traders do well with the right rule. Try again.
    none: The market can't clear without a trade.
sources:
    - id: gs1993
      title: 'Allocative Efficiency of Markets with Zero-Intelligence Traders: Market as a Partial Substitute for Individual Rationality'
      authors: Dhananjay K. Gode, Shyam Sunder
      year: 1993
      finding: In double auctions, programs bidding at random but forbidden to sell below cost or buy above value reach allocative efficiency close to 100 percent, about as high as human traders; unconstrained random traders do much worse.
    - id: cb1997
      title: 'Zero is Not Enough: On the Lower Limit of Agent Intelligence for Continuous Double Auction Markets'
      authors: Dave Cliff, Janet Bruten
      year: 1997
      finding: 'HP Labs HPL-97-91: when supply and demand are asymmetric, zero-intelligence traders'' prices stray well away from the equilibrium; traders with a simple learning rule fix it.'

    box t_human "Human traders" x 160 y 530 width 240 height 150 radius 10 icon person font_size 26 hidden true
    box t_zic "Random + one rule" cites ["gs1993"] x 420 y 530 width 240 height 150 radius 10 icon robot font_size 26 hidden true
    box t_ziu "Random, no rule" cites ["gs1993"] x 680 y 530 width 240 height 150 radius 10 icon "game die" font_size 26 hidden true
    text rule "Never buy above worth · never sell below cost" #2f6b8f x 420 y 630 font_size 24 hidden true
    text v_human "nearly all the gain" #3d3833 x 160 y 425 font_size 28 hidden true
    text v_zic "nearly all the gain" #3d3833 cites ["gs1993"] x 420 y 425 font_size 28 hidden true
    text v_ziu "much less" #b5482e cites ["gs1993"] x 680 y 425 font_size 28 hidden true
    text n_gs "Gode & Sunder, 1993" cites ["gs1993"] x 420 y 365 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Does it need clever traders?"

Four steps, and each leans on things it never says aloud, like a price that carries every cost and nobody big enough to set it. Which conditions it needs is a question of its own.
    draw t_human

B: Here's a stranger doubt. Step one pictures shrewd, self-interested traders. Does the argument even need them?
    highlight t_human

In 1993 Dhananjay Gode and Shyam Sunder ran markets whose traders had zero intelligence: computer programs that bid at random.
    draw t_zic n_gs

B: With one rule: never buy above what the thing is worth to you, never sell below your cost. Steps one and two, and nothing else.
    draw rule

    erase n_gs

    quiz "Random bids, plus that one rule. How much of the possible gain did these traders capture?" voice b
        right: Nearly all of it: close to a hundred percent, about as much as human traders in the same markets.
        wrong: The rule already rules out every losing trade: here, nearly all of it.
        * About a quarter
        * About half
        + Nearly all of it

Nearly all of it. The rule and the auction did the work; the cleverness added little.
    draw v_human v_zic parallel true

B: And random traders without the rule, free to trade a (@draw v_ziu@)t a loss, wasted much of the gain.
    draw t_ziu

So maybe Smith's hand is less about clever self-interest, and more about the rules of the market.
    highlight rule

B: But in 1997 Dave Cliff and Janet Bruten showed that when supply and demand are lopsided, random traders' prices stray far from the crossing. A little learning puts them back.
    text n_cb "Cliff & Bruten, 1997: lopsided markets" cites ["cb1997"] x 500 y 365 font_size 26

    erase n_cb

So how sure are we? That the argument holds when its conditions hold is a theorem: consensus.
    box conf1 "The logic: consensus" x 300 y 340 width 300 height 90 font_size 28

B: How much of a real market's success is the rules, and how much the people in it, is contested.
    box conf2 "Rules or brains: contested" #b5482e x 700 y 340 width 330 height 90 font_size 28

    quiz "Concert tickets resell in an open market that settles at $80 a ticket. Who ends up with the tickets?"
        right: Those who value a ticket above $80: anyone who values it less sells or doesn't buy.
        wrong: Compare each fan's worth with $80: here, those who value it more.
        * Everyone who wanted one
        + Those who value a ticket above $80
        * Those who queued first

    quiz "A competitive bread market is efficient. Does that mean every hungry family gets bread?" voice b
        right: No: the demand curve counts what people are willing and able to pay, so a family with no money counts for nothing in it.
        wrong: Whose worth does demand count? Here: no.
        * Yes: efficiency means nobody goes without
        + No: it counts what people can pay, not what they need

So why would a market ever be efficient? Because one price lines up everyone's own reasons where the total gain peaks, and the market's rules may matter as much as anyone's cleverness.
    highlight rule v_zic

